well here we are a few months into 2014  and still just a few months into the new  consul cycle one argument I keep hearing  over and over again for PC gamers is why  would you buy a next-gen console when  you can just build a PC for the same  cost and get better performance well I'm  not entirely convinced that that's  something you can do so well myself and  my colleague Mark Walden in the UK are  going to do is we're going to build a PC  for the same cost as an Xbox one in the  highest tax state United States with a  budget of about $550 we run to buy every  component you need including you know  GPU CPU mouse keyboard and windows and  see if you can actually build a PC that  will perform better than a next-gen  console without breaking your budget  here are the parts we're working with  the most obvious part to go for is the  operating system first although I have a  personal aversion to the Metro user  interface I decided to go with Windows 8  because there's a better chance  Microsoft will support it further into  the future than Windows 7 it's easy to  go overboard on a mouse but as long as  it tracks movement and has two buttons  and a scroll wheel I should be able to  play all games without a problem  I also shied away from wasting money on  a keyboard by avoiding unnecessary  features again I opted for a Rosewall  product that's functional enough to get  the job done my choice of a motherboard  is based on a few key factors first it  had to support Intel's LGA 1155 CPU  socket so that I could have a wide range  of new processors to choose from  secondly it had to feature a PCI Express  3.0 slot to ensure wasn't bottlenecking  and graphics card biostar is age 61 mg  v3 satisfied both of these needs  choosing this board came with the cost  of a few features but on the plus side  it was cheap and the micro ATX form  factor meant I could build a relatively  small machine to boot  once I established that I was building a  machine around a micro ATX board I  searched for a small and cheap case and  luckily enough I found one that came  paired with a 300 watt power supply most  gaming pcs require a bit more juice to  power high-end CPUs and GPUs but I'm  building on the cheap Nvidia's GTX 750  Ti is a mighty card for 160 bucks from  EVGA and it doesn't draw too much power  which means it'll work nicely with my  cheap power supply GPUs are the most  important item in a gaming PC build and  even though I would have liked to spent  a little bit more money on a stronger  card I couldn't really spend any more  given my budget most people default to  Intel's Core line of CPUs and building a  PC but again I'm on a budget and so long  as I have a few cores to play with I  should be in good enough shape plus it  was cheap at only $80 choosing Ram was  easy  for most of the games all detesting I  needed at least four gigabytes I don't  need it to be particularly fast though I  opted for two sticks instead of one in  order to take advantage of two memory  channels for increased bandwidth the  final piece of the puzzle is the hard  drive Western Digital blue drives are  inexpensive and reliable and I was able  to purchase a serial ata hard drive with  500 gigabytes of storage while  ultimately staying below my budget all  right that's it for the parts let's put  this PC together alright we were working  with some cramped space inside of this  little box and cable management is  almost non-existent given the  limitations of such a cheap kit but  we've got it all put together let's see  how it works with assassin's creed for  Assassin's Creed 4 proved to be the most  taxing game of the bunch forcing me to  disable motion blur ambient occlusion  and volumetric fog but it's still  performed admirably compared to the Xbox  one version rendered at 900 P and 30  frames per second frame rates on the PC  took the most noticeable dive during  synchronization points but it still  perform as good as or better than the  PlayStation 4 version which is rendered  at a fixed 30 frames per second at 1080p  after struggling to push Assassin's  Creed for his graphical settings it was  great to see our PC handle thief with  such ease granted we weren't able to max  out the settings but the game looked  great on high and maintain 60 frames per  second at 1080p blowing volt consoles  out of the water  apart from lowering a few settings that  were found attacks the CPU such as  impact marks and ragdoll physics  titanfall ran beautifully at 1080p on  high settings compared to the Xbox one  at 792p we had to disable anti-aliasing  in order to achieve high frame rates but  as a result the game hovered between 50  and 60 frames per second during normal  play and around 45 seconds during scenes  with exceptionally long draw distances  before our tests I expected battlefield  4 to be the most taxing game of the lot  but as it turned out we were able to  keep most settings on high without the  framerate dipping below 40 again we had  to avoid flourishes such as ambient  occlusion but like the rest of the games  in our test it still looked better  overall than its console counterparts  all right I admit it  I was skeptical going into this  challenge but the result speak for  themselves for $550 the highest cost of  an Xbox ball in the United States we've  built an Intel Nvidia based PC that  outperforms both the PlayStation 4 and  Xbox one in cross-platform games and if  we had a little bit more money we could  have done just a few components would  have made a much larger difference and  it goes without saying PC gaming library  is huge and we think of all the other  things a PC can do that consoles can't  do the value speaks for itself  but as it stands from a gaming  perspective we've built a PC that  outperforms both next-gen consoles for  the highest price of the Xbox point for  GameSpot I'm Peter Brown see you next  time
 

